<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<bpmn:definitions xmlns:bpmn="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL" xmlns:bpmndi="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/DI" xmlns:dc="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DC" xmlns:di="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DI" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:color="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/non-normative/color/1.0" id="Definitions_quiz" targetNamespace="http://si212.umich.edu/schema/bpmn" exporter="Camunda Modeler" exporterVersion="5.50.1">
  <bpmn:collaboration id="C_quiz">
    <bpmn:participant id="P_day" name="Quiz day: one run per quiz date" processRef="Proc_day" />
    <bpmn:participant id="P_student" name="A student taking the quiz in class">
      <bpmn:documentation>A black box on purpose. What happens inside is the web app and the student, and this diagram makes no claim about it beyond the two messages. The three bars say there is one run of this per student, and the run of Quiz day does not wait for any of them: the quiz closes on the clock, which is why the export has to carry drafts as well as submissions.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:participantMultiplicity minimum="1" />
    </bpmn:participant>
    <bpmn:messageFlow id="m_serve" name="the draw, one per student" sourceRef="T_open" targetRef="P_student" />
    <bpmn:messageFlow id="m_answers" name="answers and drafts" sourceRef="P_student" targetRef="T_export" />
  </bpmn:collaboration>
  <bpmn:process id="Proc_day" name="Quiz day" isExecutable="false">
    <bpmn:laneSet id="LS_day">
      <bpmn:lane id="L_human" name="HUMAN operator">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Start_day</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_curate</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_publish</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_pool</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_open</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_close</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_review</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>G_rejoin</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>G_criteria</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
      <bpmn:lane id="L_agent" name="AI AGENT">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_draft</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Sub_grade</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Sub_regrade</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
      <bpmn:lane id="L_program" name="PROGRAM">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_bake</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_seed</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_export</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_queue</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_merge</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_comments</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_push</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_scores</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
    </bpmn:laneSet>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f1" sourceRef="Start_day" targetRef="T_draft" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f2" sourceRef="T_draft" targetRef="T_curate" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f3" sourceRef="T_curate" targetRef="T_publish" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f4" sourceRef="T_publish" targetRef="T_pool" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f5" sourceRef="T_pool" targetRef="T_bake" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f6" sourceRef="T_bake" targetRef="T_seed" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f7" sourceRef="T_seed" targetRef="T_open" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f8" sourceRef="T_open" targetRef="T_close" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f9" sourceRef="T_close" targetRef="T_export" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f10" sourceRef="T_export" targetRef="T_queue" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="q1" sourceRef="T_queue" targetRef="Sub_grade" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f11" sourceRef="Sub_grade" targetRef="T_merge" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="q2" sourceRef="T_merge" targetRef="G_rejoin" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="q3" sourceRef="G_rejoin" targetRef="T_review" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f12" sourceRef="T_review" targetRef="G_criteria" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="q4" name="yes" sourceRef="G_criteria" targetRef="Sub_regrade">
      <bpmn:conditionExpression xsi:type="bpmn:tFormalExpression">the reviewer wrote a note saying what should change</bpmn:conditionExpression>
    </bpmn:sequenceFlow>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="q5" sourceRef="Sub_regrade" targetRef="G_rejoin" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="q6" name="no" sourceRef="G_criteria" targetRef="T_comments" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="q7" sourceRef="T_comments" targetRef="T_push" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f13" sourceRef="T_push" targetRef="End_scores" />
    <bpmn:startEvent id="Start_day" name="calendar names&#10;a quiz date">
      <bpmn:documentation>The instructor's term calendar is the only file that knows a date. It names which session carries a quiz and what that quiz is on.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f1</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:startEvent>
    <bpmn:serviceTask id="T_draft" name="Draft bank items">
      <bpmn:documentation>Overproduced deliberately: about forty items drafted for twenty to twenty-five kept. There is no skill file for this; it is an ordinary drafting conversation against the source's goals and activities. The draft lands in the source's tasks/ and rubrics/ on a branch.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f1</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f2</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:serviceTask>
    <bpmn:userTask id="T_curate" name="Curate the bank">
      <bpmn:documentation>The instructor keeps the items worth asking and cuts the rest. This is the step that makes a drafted bank an examinable one, and nothing adjudicates it but the instructor.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f2</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f3</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:userTask>
    <bpmn:userTask id="T_publish" name="Publish the bank to students">
      <bpmn:documentation>The items are public and the selection is private. Publishing is a release and follows whichever route the source uses: a learning topic is merged and pushed as topic release does it, and an assignment follow-up bank is pushed with the assignment. Students then have every question they could be asked, which is what makes studying the source and studying for the quiz the same act.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f3</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f4</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:userTask>
    <bpmn:userTask id="T_pool" name="Write the session&#39;s pool file">
      <bpmn:documentation>quiz-bank/session-&lt;n&gt;.pool.json, in this repository and published. It names the sources and how many to draw from each of their tasks files. Publishing it is what makes practice the real thing rather than a rehearsal of it: the student's draw comes out of the same file, through the same library. It discloses nothing, because it says how many from where and never which questions, and the questions themselves are in repositories students already have.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f4</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f5</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:userTask>
    <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_bake" name="Bake a draw for every student&#10;on the roster&#10;quiz-bake.mjs">
      <bpmn:documentation>umsi212F2026/course-private:tools/quiz-bake.mjs, instructor only, not in this repository. It reads the pool file, resolves each source to a folder with tasks/ and rubrics/, and draws per student. The roster comes from that date's seating chart; there is no second roster source. The draw is deterministic, seeded from the date and the uniqname, so fixing a typo and re-baking does not reshuffle everyone's quiz. It shares its draw library with the practice front end.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f5</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f6</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:scriptTask>
    <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_seed" name="Seed the draws into the app&#10;quiz-seed.mjs">
      <bpmn:documentation>umsi212F2026/course-private:tools/quiz-seed.mjs, instructor only, not in this repository. An upsert keyed on date and uniqname, so re-baking after a fix is just running it again. Refused once the quiz is open unless forced, because the same upsert would replace items under students who are mid-quiz.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f6</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f7</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:scriptTask>
    <bpmn:userTask id="T_open" name="Open the quiz">
      <bpmn:documentation>One button on the app's admin page. The answer and the rubric are stripped on the student path, so a draw that carries its own answers cannot be read out of the page.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f7</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f8</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:userTask>
    <bpmn:userTask id="T_close" name="Close the quiz">
      <bpmn:documentation>On the clock, not on everyone having finished. That is why this run never waits on the student pool, and why the export has to carry drafts: a student who typed answers and never pressed Submit is a third state, distinct from one who submitted and one who did nothing.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f8</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f9</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:userTask>
    <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_export" name="Export the submissions&#10;quiz-export.mjs">
      <bpmn:documentation>umsi212F2026/course-private:tools/quiz-export.mjs, instructor only, not in this repository. Writes data/quizzes/&lt;date&gt;-submissions.json: the latest submission per student, plus a drafts array for answers that were typed and never submitted. The grader reads this file and never reads the database.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f9</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f10</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:scriptTask>
    <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_queue" name="Gather the answers&#10;by question&#10;quiz-grade.mjs --queue">
      <bpmn:documentation>umsi212F2026/course-private:tools/quiz-grade.mjs, instructor only, not in this repository. Settles every multiple choice in code and gathers the free answers into a queue GROUPED BY QUESTION rather than by student. That grouping is a grading decision, not a convenience: ruling every answer to one question together holds its standard still across the twenty people who answered it, where going student by student re-derives the standard once per script and drifts.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f10</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>q1</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:scriptTask>
    <bpmn:subProcess id="Sub_grade" name="Grade one answer&#10;quiz/grade">
      <bpmn:documentation>workflows/quiz/skills/quiz/grade/SKILL.md. One set of grading instructions, two runners. The batch runner here is the instructor's quiz-grade.mjs; the practice runner is the student's own agent, following the same skill in a fresh context. Consistency comes from the recorded rubric rather than from which model runs it. Called a second time from workflows/learn/learn.bpmn, where the practice quiz's Rule every written answer names this same skill. It is a task there rather than a second copy of this plane, so there is one drawing of the phase and nothing to keep in step by hand.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>q1</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f11</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:multiInstanceLoopCharacteristics isSequential="true" />
      <bpmn:laneSet id="LS_grade">
        <bpmn:lane id="L_g_human" name="HUMAN operator">
          <bpmn:documentation>Empty on purpose. Nobody is in the loop on one answer. The instructor reviews whole scripts one level up, after every answer has a credit, which is a different act from adjudicating an item.</bpmn:documentation>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="L_g_agent" name="AI AGENT">
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_judge</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="L_g_program" name="PROGRAM">
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Start_g</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>G_kind</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_mcq</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>G_gjoin</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_g</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
      </bpmn:laneSet>
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="g1" sourceRef="Start_g" targetRef="G_kind" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="g2" name="mcq" sourceRef="G_kind" targetRef="T_mcq">
        <bpmn:conditionExpression xsi:type="bpmn:tFormalExpression">the rubric entry says type: mcq</bpmn:conditionExpression>
      </bpmn:sequenceFlow>
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="g3" name="free" sourceRef="G_kind" targetRef="T_judge" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="g4" sourceRef="T_mcq" targetRef="G_gjoin" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="g5" sourceRef="T_judge" targetRef="G_gjoin" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="g6" sourceRef="G_gjoin" targetRef="End_g" />
      <bpmn:startEvent id="Start_g" name="an answer&#10;to grade">
        <bpmn:outgoing>g1</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:startEvent>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="G_kind" name="what kind&#10;of item?" default="g3">
        <bpmn:incoming>g1</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>g2</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:outgoing>g3</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_mcq" name="Compare the chosen text">
        <bpmn:documentation>Not a model call, in either runner. The app stores the chosen option as text, so an mcq is right when that text equals the answer. Both runners do this in code, which is what makes multiple choice cost nothing and never disagree with itself.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>g2</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>g4</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:serviceTask id="T_judge" name="Judge against the rubric&#10;quiz/grade">
        <bpmn:documentation>workflows/quiz/skills/quiz/grade/SKILL.md. Rules on one answer to one item, given the prompt, its rubric entry and the answer. Returns the credit (full, half or none), what the answer missed, the two axes for the log, and a flag when the credit is close enough to be worth a person's eye. Nothing branches on the flag yet: the instructor reviews every script this term, which is the experiment that says whether the flag can be trusted later.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>g3</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>g5</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:serviceTask>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="G_gjoin">
        <bpmn:incoming>g4</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:incoming>g5</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>g6</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="End_g" name="credit settled">
        <bpmn:incoming>g6</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
    </bpmn:subProcess>
    <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_merge" name="Merge the verdicts&#10;into grades&#10;quiz-grade.mjs --merge">
      <bpmn:documentation>umsi212F2026/course-private:tools/quiz-grade.mjs, instructor only, not in this repository. Folds the verdicts back onto every student and scores them. An answered item with no verdict is reported as a PROBLEM rather than scored zero: a zero is a claim about the student and a missing verdict is a claim about the grading run, and quietly turning the second into the first is how a grading bug reaches a gradebook looking like a result.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f11</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>q2</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:scriptTask>
    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="G_rejoin">
      <bpmn:incoming>q2</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:incoming>q5</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>q3</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="G_criteria" name="were the criteria&#10;wrong?" default="q6">
      <bpmn:incoming>f12</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>q4</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>q6</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
    <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_comments" name="Build the feedback&#10;quiz-comments.mjs">
      <bpmn:documentation>umsi212F2026/course-private:tools/quiz-comments.mjs, instructor only, not in this repository. One block per question: what was asked, what they wrote, what was being looked for, the feedback where there is any, and the score. The model answer is quoted because the items live in a repository students already have, so it gives nothing away and it is the part someone who scored full marks still learns from. No model runs here.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>q6</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>q7</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:scriptTask>
    <bpmn:subProcess id="Sub_regrade" name="Rewrite the criteria and regrade all answers to question&#10;quiz/grade">
      <bpmn:documentation>umsi212F2026/course-private:tools/quiz-regrade.mjs, instructor only, not in this repository. Drains the requests the review tool records, ONE AT A TIME behind a lock, because two agents committing in the same repository collide on .git/index.lock and one of them simply fails. Requests can arrive whenever the reviewer makes them; they are drained in order. The reviewer carries on with other questions while this runs, and marking is held only on the question being re-ruled.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>q4</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>q5</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:laneSet id="LS_regrade">
        <bpmn:lane id="L_r_human" name="HUMAN operator">
          <bpmn:documentation>Empty on purpose, and that emptiness is the phase's whole point. The reviewer wrote a note and moved to another question; nothing in here waits on them. It is also why the agent below gets Read, Edit and Write and nothing else: no shell and no git, because an unattended agent rewriting course material and re-marking students is enough authority without the ability to run commands too.</bpmn:documentation>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="L_r_agent" name="AI AGENT">
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_rewrite</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_rerule</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="L_r_program" name="PROGRAM">
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Start_r</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_claim</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>G_cover</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_commit</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_remerge</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>T_rollback</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_regraded</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_refused</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
      </bpmn:laneSet>
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="r1" sourceRef="Start_r" targetRef="T_claim" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="r2" sourceRef="T_claim" targetRef="T_rewrite" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="r3" sourceRef="T_rewrite" targetRef="T_rerule" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="r4" sourceRef="T_rerule" targetRef="G_cover" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="r5" name="every answer ruled" sourceRef="G_cover" targetRef="T_commit">
        <bpmn:conditionExpression xsi:type="bpmn:tFormalExpression">the new verdicts cover every answer to the question, each with one of the three credits and a timestamp</bpmn:conditionExpression>
      </bpmn:sequenceFlow>
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="r6" name="anything missing" sourceRef="G_cover" targetRef="T_rollback" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="r7" sourceRef="T_commit" targetRef="T_remerge" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="r8" sourceRef="T_remerge" targetRef="End_regraded" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="r9" sourceRef="T_rollback" targetRef="End_refused" />
      <bpmn:startEvent id="Start_r" name="a regrade&#10;requested">
        <bpmn:documentation>Recorded by the reviewer in the review tool, which shows them the exact prompt this agent will be given before anything is spent. A question whose rubric is not on main is refused here rather than attempted: its criteria were never released, so rewriting them would change a standard nobody was held to.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:outgoing>r1</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:startEvent>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_claim" name="Claim the request&#10;quiz-regrade.mjs">
        <bpmn:documentation>Refuses outright if the rubric file has uncommitted changes, because rolling back means restoring it from git and that would throw away the reviewer's own edits. Refusing beats being clever.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>r1</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>r2</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:serviceTask id="T_rewrite" name="Rewrite the credit line">
        <bpmn:documentation>Changes that one item's credit line and nothing else: not the model answer, not another item, not another file. An ambiguous note FAILS rather than guessing, because a guess changes real marks for real students with nobody watching.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>r2</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>r3</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:serviceTask>
      <bpmn:serviceTask id="T_rerule" name="Re-rule every answer&#10;quiz/grade">
        <bpmn:documentation>workflows/quiz/skills/quiz/grade/SKILL.md, the same skill that ruled them the first time. Every answer, including those already at full credit: the standard has moved, and a mark made under the old one is not evidence about the new one. An answer the reviewer had corrected by hand is re-ruled too, flagged, and quoted back to them, because the correction was made against criteria that no longer exist. The student is never told any of this happened.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>r3</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>r4</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:multiInstanceLoopCharacteristics isSequential="true" />
      </bpmn:serviceTask>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="G_cover" name="do the verdicts&#10;cover it?" default="r6">
        <bpmn:incoming>r4</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>r5</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:outgoing>r6</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_commit" name="Commit the rubric,&#10;never push">
        <bpmn:documentation>The runner commits, not the agent, and only after the check above passes. A commit is a record and a push is a release, and a background process does not make releases.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>r5</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>r7</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_remerge" name="Merge the new verdicts&#10;quiz-grade.mjs --merge">
        <bpmn:documentation>New verdicts that never reach the grades file are invisible: the review tool reads grades, so a finished regrade would show the old marks and look as though it had done nothing.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>r7</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>r8</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_rollback" name="Put the rubric and&#10;the verdicts back">
        <bpmn:documentation>A PARTIAL REGRADE IS WORSE THAN NONE, and it is invisible: the status says failed so nobody looks, while some students sit re-ruled under criteria the rest were not. Every failure after the agent is allowed to write truncates the verdicts back and restores the rubric from git.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>r6</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>r9</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="End_regraded" name="criteria rewritten,&#10;answers re-ruled">
        <bpmn:incoming>r8</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="End_refused" name="nothing changed">
        <bpmn:incoming>r9</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
    </bpmn:subProcess>
    <bpmn:userTask id="T_review" name="Review the graded scripts&#10;quiz-review.mjs">
      <bpmn:documentation>Every script, every week, until the flag has earned trust. There is no gateway in front of this on purpose: nothing is filtered out yet. When the flag is trusted, this becomes a branch and the label narrows to the flagged items.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>q3</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f12</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:userTask>
    <bpmn:scriptTask id="T_push" name="Push the scores to Canvas&#10;quiz-scores.mjs">
      <bpmn:documentation>umsi212F2026/course-private:tools/quiz-scores.mjs, instructor only, not in this repository. One bulk call against the gradebook, keyed on the canvas_id the seating chart already carries.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>q7</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f13</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:scriptTask>
    <bpmn:endEvent id="End_scores" name="scores in&#10;the gradebook">
      <bpmn:incoming>f13</bpmn:incoming>
    </bpmn:endEvent>
  </bpmn:process>
